{"seq": 8, "at": "2026-09-13T11:15:09Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "idea", "text": "Payoff-irrelevant rebate repair preserving Emmy operator: write \\(k_N=(N^2-N+1)/(N-1)^2\\), \\(\\bar p_{-n}^{av}=(N-1)^{-1}\\sum_{m\\ne n}p_m\\). Add \\(r_n=\\alpha(k_N-1)(\\bar p_{-n}^{av})^\\top[\\sum_{m\\ne n}x_m-(N-1)c/N]\\) to Q tax. It depends only on opponents. At any common-price capacity-complementary equilibrium, new tax becomes \\(\\lambda^\\top(x_n-c/N)\\), with \\(\\lambda=\\alpha p\\). Hence BB holds and concavity plus planner KKT gives IR for normalized valuations with \\(0\\in\\mathcal X_n\\). The game and its PBR dynamics are unchanged, but Q published P3/P4 coefficient certificates need replacement.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 9, "at": "2026-09-13T11:15:48Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "text": "Read #8 and independently confirm Ada rebate repair exactly; rational-arithmetic evaluation gives original taxes \\((21/2,-21/4,-21/4)\\), corrected taxes \\((6,-3,-3)\\), and corrected utilities \\((7/2,3,3)\\) in #7. The proposed weighted operator is not yet a proof of Q PBR nonexpansiveness. I will write the weighted monotonicity/all-equilibria theorem rigorously, keeping algorithmic convergence separate. For CVaR, samplewise \\(m\\)-strong convexity of losses suffices to preserve \\(m\\) under population CVaR, empirical CVaR, expectation, and ball smoothing; Q expected strong concavity alone does not suffice.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 10, "at": "2026-09-13T11:16:56Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "text": "Further direct check: Q Assumption 5 (Euclidean nonexpansiveness of PBR) is not automatic even for correctly signed quadratic valuations. For \\(N=3\\), curvature and tax scale \\(m=\\alpha=1\\), an interior consensus perturbation yields \\(T_{\\rm cons}=\\begin{psmallmatrix}\\mu+1&-1/2\\\\-1/2&\\mu+1\\end{psmallmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{psmallmatrix}\\mu&-3/2\\\\1&\\mu+1\\end{psmallmatrix}\\). Its price column is \\((-(\\mu+1)/D,1-1/(2D))\\), \\(D=(\\mu+1)^2-1/4\\), whose squared norm is \\(1+1/(2D^2)>1\\) for every \\(\\mu>0\\). Local interior examples therefore violate Assumption 5 for all proximal parameters. This does not show damped iterations diverge, but rules out carrying over that certificate; weighted monotonicity alone does not prove Q PBR convergence.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 11, "at": "2026-09-13T11:18:00Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "idea", "text": "Constructive dynamics alternative to unsupported PBR: for fixed batch size and smoothing, P eq:surrogate-unbiased-gradient supplies an unbiased bounded oracle for the surrogate weighted monotone operator \\(\\widetilde G\\). On a compact strategy product containing a planner KKT equilibrium, use outer resolvent \\(R(z)=(I+\\gamma(\\widetilde G+N_{\\mathcal S}))^{-1}z\\). Each inner problem has strongly monotone operator \\(\\widetilde G(s)+\\gamma^{-1}(s-z)\\), solved by projected stochastic gradient using P local oracle and analytic payment gradients with aggregate messages. A standard recurrence gives conditional mean-square inner error \\(C/(T_k+1)\\). Taking \\(T_k=\\lceil(k+1)^{2+\\epsilon}\\rceil\\) makes expected norm errors summable, so inexact proximal-point iterates converge a.s. and in mean square (compactness) to a surrogate equilibrium; allocations have a deterministic unique limit. I will supply the elementary proof, not cite this as inherited from Q. Query-neighborhood access is an explicit extra assumption if retaining \\(0\\in\\mathcal X_n\\); P inward shrink alone can exclude the IR outside-option deviation.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 12, "at": "2026-09-13T11:18:00Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "text": "Prior-work check weakens novelty of generic CVaR-in-games proposal. Queries run: \"risk-averse\" \"zeroth-order\" games CVaR Wang 2022; \"mechanism design\" \"budget balance\" \"CVaR\" Nash; \"proximal best-response\" \"monotone\" convergence games. Found Wang, Shen, Zavlanos (ICML 2022), https://proceedings.mlr.press/v162/wang22w.html, already using sampled empirical CVaR and one-point gradients in noncooperative games with regret guarantees; and Cui, Shanbhag, https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.07860, covering stochastic proximal-point methods for monotone games and smoothing-based approximate NE in a different hierarchical setting. Hence novelty cannot rest on the estimator or generic proximal convergence. The specific contribution would be corrected incentive-compatible risk-surrogate implementation with explicit welfare/IR error bounds and the price-consensus obstruction. The mechanism query did not establish absence of prior work.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 13, "at": "2026-09-13T11:18:14Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "text": "Proved a sharper fixed-surrogate error bound in ada/research-note.md: \\(H_{n,s}=\\mathbb E\\widehat C_{n,s}\\le C_n\\) by infimum/expectation, while P DKW gives \\(C_n-e_n\\le H_{n,s}\\), \\(e_n=(U_n/\\beta_n)\\sqrt{2\\pi/s_n}\\). Centered convex smoothing therefore gives \\(-e_n\\le\\widetilde C_n-C_n\\le L_n\\delta_n\\). Error oscillation \\(w_n=e_n+L_n\\delta_n\\) controls unilateral actual regret and actual IR shortfall by \\(w_n\\), welfare loss by \\(\\sum_nw_n\\), and squared allocation error by \\(2\\sum_nw_n/m\\), at an exactly implemented surrogate equilibrium. BB stays exact. This removes P asymptotic inverse-radius factor; P itself gets \\(O(\\delta+s^{-1/2})\\) after its domain-comparator term. No uniform random empirical-process bound is needed because the expected surrogate is deterministic.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 14, "at": "2026-09-13T11:19:50Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "text": "Cross-checks: Emmy #4 weighted Schur threshold is correct after consensus/disagreement decomposition; #10 PBR consensus-column identity is correct, and ada/check_algebra.py checks it with exact fractions. CVaR creates a sharper price warning even at interior allocations: \\(N=3,\\gamma=1,\\mathcal X_n=[0,1],c=3/2\\), iid equiprobable \\(Z=\\pm1\\), \\(J_n(x,Z)=x^2-3x+Z(x-1/2)\\), tail \\(\\beta=1/2\\). Then \\(C_n=x^2-3x+|x-1/2|\\), unique planner allocation \\(x_n=1/2\\), but every common price \\(p=\\lambda\\in[1,3]\\) satisfies NE KKT. This example has samplewise strong convexity 2 and all allocations interior. Thus CVaR kink can create price nonuniqueness even where smooth interior stationarity would identify a price. Allocation convergence is the appropriate robust target.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
